WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.920
The number of countries in the United Nations has grown over the decades,

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.160
from 51 States in 1945 to 193 today.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.440
Many more places want to become independent,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.960
but national governments almost always oppose secession.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.200
Catalonia's Parliament declared independence from Spain in October 2017

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:28.000
following a referendum deemed illegal by the central authorities in Madrid.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.060
Spain dismissed the region's government

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.840
and decreed that a fresh election should be held.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:40.600
Catalan politicians leading the independence movement could be jailed for decades if found guilty of rebellion and sedition.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.840
The autonomous Kurdish region in Iraq also held a recent independence referendum,

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:52.780
it outraged the government in Baghdad and neighboring countries, which also have Kurdish minorities.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:00.960
The Baghdad government claimed the independence referendum was illegal and seized back the lands Kurds had taken beyond their region.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.480
The desire for secession is nothing new,

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:10.380
there are estimated to be more than 8,000 ethno-cultural groups in the world,

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.000
with many independence movements demanding their homelands be recognized as countries.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:24.980
Jean-Claude Juncker, the President of the European Commission said that: "If every region had its way the EU would become unmanageable."

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.840
So who gets to form a state?

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.880
There are no clear international rules.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.680
The 1933 Montevideo Convention

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.240
declared that a region needs four things to become a state:

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:46.920
a permanent population, a defined territory, a government and the capacity to enter into relations with other states.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.280
But these are only guidelines,

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.140
ideally, a region would also need other conditions, such as:

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.760
show that a clear majority have freely chosen independence,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:04.740
respect its own minorities, have a viable state and settle terms with the state that leaves and its neighbors.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.580
In practice, all of these conditions are hard to meet.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.700
Taiwan is a modern democratic state in all but international law.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:19.000
It's an island off the southeastern coast of China which has its own constitution,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:27.240
functions as an independent state, has democratically elected leaders and was the first place in Asia to rule in favor of gay marriage.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:31.840
Per head it's around three times richer than mainland China.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.580
But China regards Taiwan as a renegade province,

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:42.780
it insists that no country can have diplomatic ties with both itself and Taiwan.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.760
Only 19 countries, plus the Vatican, officially recognize it.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.300
Somaliland is another region seeking to be recognized as a country,

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.080
the semi-desert territory on the coast of the Gulf of Aden

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.620
declared independence from Somalia in 1991.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:05.020
It has its own government, police force and currency,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.160
but no foreign government recognizes it,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.580
perhaps for good reason...

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.380
To recognize Somaliland would encourage other separatists in the region,

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:22.840
it would undermine the already weak federal government of Somalia, and it would probably lead to war.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:31.100
There have only been two major changes to African borders since the 1960s and neither is a success story.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.260
Eritrea declared independence from Ethiopia in 1993.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:43.360
Today, Eritrea has a despotic government and its people are mired in abject poverty.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:50.380
In 2011, South Sudan seceded from Sudan, making it the world's newest country.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:57.540
Soon after South Sudan formed, civil war broke out, displacing 2.2 million people.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:07.520
Although a ceasefire was signed in 2015, the country lies in ruin, plagued by hyperinflation, famine and violence.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.320
Independence has a romantic ring, many sympathize with the demand for freedom,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.360
but redrawing borders is dangerous and often causes as many problems as it solves.

